# ===========================================================================
# ONE KIND OF LINE ENDING IN THE REPOSITORY, AND THE SAME KIND ON DISK.
#
# Phase 12 Section 4, Section 0, debt four. Operator ruling 2026-09-09, after
# the board on MAIN failed on a check that had passed on the feature branch
# with no change to the code between them.
#
# WHAT HAPPENED
# --------------
# retention-audit asserts that the one DELETE in ops-retention.ts sits inside
# the mode check, with a pattern spanning two lines. Node had written that file
# with LF while it was being worked on; `git checkout main` materialised the
# same bytes with CRLF; the pattern stopped matching. The check was measuring
# how the file had arrived on disk rather than what the code says.
#
# A check whose answer depends on that is worse than one that is merely wrong.
# It passes for one person and fails for the next with nothing to argue about
# between them.
#
# WHY BOTH HALVES
# ----------------
# scripts/lib/read-source.mjs normalises at the read, and it only saves the
# audits that use it. This file makes the working tree match what every patch
# script in this repository already writes, so the next reader who reaches for
# readFileSync is not saved by luck.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text. `eol=lf` says that text is checked
# out with LF on every platform, including this one.
# ===========================================================================

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, declared so git never tries to normalise them. A renormalisation that
# rewrote a PNG would be a quiet corruption rather than a noisy one.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.zip  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.webm binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- evidence CSVs
#
# CRLF, and deliberately against the eol=lf rule above.
#
# src/lib/csv.ts writes CRLF because the people who open these are on Windows
# with Excel, where a lone newline turns the whole file into one long row. The
# blanket rule would normalise that to LF on commit and check it back out broken,
# which would make the evidence pack unreadable in the one application anybody
# will actually open it in.
#
# This is the narrow exception to the line ending law recorded in BACKLOG, and it
# is narrow on purpose: one directory, one extension.
evidence/*.csv text eol=crlf
